Accession Number  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status  Amend Addenda Type  Surgical Pathology Specimen Type  Consult material Procedure Date  Ordering Provider  ROGER L CHRISTIAN M D  CASE  BS 06 REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID PATIENT  REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME            Addended Report             Resident  Peter M Sadow  M D   Ph D  Pathologist  Susan Lester  M D   Ph D  PATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS  CONSULT SLIDES FROM MASSACHUSETTS GENERAL HOSPITAL  BOSTON  MA  RIGHT BREAST  TWO EXCISIONAL BIOPSIES  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  REMOVED_DATE    WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR CALCIFICATIONS  EXTENSIVE COLUMNAR CELL CHANGE WITH FLAT ATYPIA  associated with calcifications  is present in 5 of 7 blocks  see note below   The columnar cell changes are present   REMOVED_DATE cm from all margins except for the lateral margin  An en face section of the lateral margin is not involved  Rare micropapillae and a single arch are present in 2 of the 5 blocks  blocks REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  levels examined   RE EXCISION  EXTENSIVE COLUMNAR CELL CHANGE WITH FLAT ATYPIA  associated with calcifications  is present in 8 of 8 blocks  see note below   By report  the cells are immunoreactive for ER and PR  block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Columnar cell changes are present at the posterior  anterior  and inferior margins    REMOVED_DATE cm from the superior and lateral margins  and REMOVED_DATE to 0 2 cm from the medial margin  Rare micropapillae and small cribriform spaces are focally present in 3 of the 8 blocks  blocks REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  levels examined   Atypical lobular hyperplasia is present  Note  Although the columnar cell changes are extensive in both biopsies and associated with nuclear atypia  the scant very focal architectural changes  after complete microscopic examination of both specimens and numerous levels  favors classifying this lesion as ATYPICAL DUCTAL HYPERPLASIA  Selected slides from this case were also reviewed by Dr  Deborah Dillon and Dr  Stuart Schnitt  who concur with this interpretation  Clinical correlation is suggested to ensure that all radiologic suspicious calcifications have been excised  CLINICAL DATA  History  None given Clinical Diagnosis  DCIS TISSUE SUBMITTED  Consult slides  GROSS DESCRIPTION  Received from Massachusetts General Hospital  Pathology Department  Boston  MA REMOVED_CASE_ID  are thirty  30  glass slides  The slides are labeled  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  and sublabeled  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  through  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  through  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  through  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  through  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  through  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  through  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   corresponding to A  right breast excisional biopsy  4 0 x 2 0 x REMOVED_DATE cm  B  right breast re excision  4 1 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm  performed on 9 20 06  according to the accompanying pathology report bearing the patient s name  By his her signature below  the senior physician certifies that he she personally conducted a microscopic examination   gross only  exam if so stated  of the described specimen s  and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis es  related thereto  Final Diagnosis by Susan Lester M D   Ph D   Electronically signed on 10 11 2006 SURGICAL ADDENDUM  This case was also reviewed by Dr  Lee K  Tan at Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  REMOVED_DATE   and the text of her letter to Dr  Melinda Lerwill is as follows   I have now shown this difficult and challenging case to our breast pathology group  including Dr  Edi Brogi  As I expected  our opinions range from extensive atypical columnar cell changes flat epithelial atypia to ductal carcinoma in situ  While we all agree that there are foci in these excisions  particularly in the re excision specimen  that would cytologically qualify for flat clinging ductal carcinoma in situ as described by Dr  Azzopardi  we also know that these lesions follow a very indolent clinical course as reported by Eusebi et al  Coincidentally  I met Dr  Ian Ellis who was at the same meeting I attended a couple of days ago  and one of his lectures was about columnar cell lesions  Apparently  he and his colleagues have a paper that has been accepted for publication in the American Journal of Surgical Pathology on this subject  They did a molecular study of a spectrum of columnar cell changes including flat epithelial atypia and low grade  micropapillary  DCIS  and showed   not surprisingly   that there is a continuum  In addition  he also mentioned that when a retrospective re examination of the slides from the European trial of DCIS  they found cases of DCIS associated with flat epithelial atypia at the periphery  with some cases involving margins  and that they did not find an increased number of recurrences or increased risk for development of invasive carcinoma in this subset of patients  Dr  Ellis  observations seem to corroborate Dr  Eusebi s publication regarding the indolent biological behavior of these lesions  Taking the above findings in consideration and realizing that opinions will vary regarding these borderline epithelial proliferations  our final consensus is that it is probably best to classify the changes seen in this case as  columnar cell changes with atypia flat epithelial atypia   Given the extensive nature of the lesion in this case  however  we would recommend re excision of residual calcifications if clinically indicated and very close clinical follow up   Addendum  1 by Susan Lester M D   Ph D   Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE   report_end , 0.
