Accession Number  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status  Final Type  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID BREAST CORE NEEDLE BIOPSY IES  Accessioned On  REMOVED_DATE  KUTOK  JEFFREY  LORNE  M D  PH D  DIAGNOSIS  by KUTOK JEFFREY LORNE M D  PH D  LEFT BREAST  12 00  14G ULTRASOUND GUIDED CORE NEEDLE BIOPSY FOR A CIRCUMSCRIBED LOBULATED MASS  0 7 CM   BREAST TISSUE WITH ADENOSIS AND ASSOCIATED MICROCALCIFICATIONS AND A DENSE LYMPHOID INFILTRATE The core sections show breast parenchyma with prominent lymphocytic infiltrate composed of small cells with round to occasionally irregular nuclei  coarse chromatin  inconspicuous nucleoli and scant cytoplasm  Scattered admixed plasma cells and occasional larger cells are noted  Focally the lymphoid infiltrate is present within the ductal epithelium  Immunoperoxidase studies performed on formalin fixed paraffin sections show a decreased extent of lymphoid infiltrate compared to the H   E sections that are composed of a mixed population of B cells  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID positive  and T cells  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID positive  with slight predominance of the T cell population  No aberrant expression of the T cell markers REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID or REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID is seen in the B cell population  Rare cells are positive for REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID highlights breast epithelium and myoepithelial cells and is negative in the lymphoid cells  NOTE  Complete evaluation of the lymphoid infiltrate is made difficult by the patchy nature of the process  the overall specimen sample size and the fact that the infiltrate is considerably smaller on levels used for immunohistochemical studies  While the prominence of the infiltrate and focal epithelial involvement raises the possibility of involvement by a B cell lymphoproliferative disorder  diagnostic features of a lymphoproliferative disorder are not seen in the immunophenotypic studies  If clinical concern remains or the lesion persists  progresses  or new lesions develop  re biopsy with fresh tissue for ancillary hematopathology studies is suggested  if indicated  In addition  correlation with clinical and radiologic features to ensure that these findings are representative of the underlying lesion is suggested  CLINICAL DATA  History  Left 7 mm mass  12 00  Operation  14G ultrasound  Operative findings  Circumscribed lobulated mass  TISSUE SUBMITTED  Left breast core samples  Cassette   pink  GROSS DESCRIPTION  by BROWNE TARA JANE MB BCH BAO The specimen is received fixed in one part  labeled with the patient s name  unit number and  left breast  and consists of a single pink cassette containing multiple fragments of pink tan yellow fibroadipose tissue  measuring REMOVED_DATE to REMOVED_DATE  cm in diameter and REMOVED_DATE to REMOVED_DATE cm in length  Three of the cores are large measuring over REMOVED_DATE cm in length  The specimen is entirely submitted  Micro 1  breast cores  ESS  fsd Reports to  MEYER  JACK  E  M D  SPECIMEN TYPE  BREAST CORE NEEDLE BIOPSY IES   report_end , 0.
Accession Number  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status  FinalType  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID OPERATION DATE  19 REMOVED_MONTH 00 ACCESSIONED ON  19 REMOVED_MONTH 00 at 13 07 CLINICAL DATA  42 year old with mass right breast 6 o clock    fibroadenoma                                                                                     FINAL DIAGNOSIS     BREAST  RIGHT   ULTRASOUND GUIDED NEEDLE CORE BIOPSY  1  MINOR FIBROCYTIC CHANGES  WITH PSEUDOANGIOMATOUS STROMAL HYPERPLASIA AND RARE MICROCALCIFICATIONS  2  THERE IS NO EVIDENCE OF MALIGNANCY  Diagnosis by  John H  Eichhorn  M D  Signed On  22 REMOVED_MONTH 00 at 21 12                                                                                 GROSS DESCRIPTION  Received in formalin  labeled with the patient s name and unit number only  with no designation as to site  are eight cores of white pink  fibroadipose tissue ranging in size from REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm to REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm which are submitted in toto in A  Gross Description by  Robert F Donnell Resident  ELENA BRACHTEL  MD                                                                                 SOURCE CARE UNIT  MGH OUTPATIENT  OFF P CLINIC  REPORTS TO  Marcia Ann Zucker  M D  Priscilla Slanetz  M D  PART   BREAST  RIGHT   CORE BIOSPY, 0.
Accession Number  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID REMOVED_CASE_ID Report Status  Signed Type  Surgical Pathology Procedure Date  REMOVED_DATE  DIAGNOSIS  Left breast  needle biopsy at 12 o clock  Carcinoma in situ  indeterminate whether lobular or ductal  Foreign body reaction with cholesterol clefts  NOTE  E Cadherin stain is mixed positive and negative in the carcinoma in situ  The case was referred to Dr  Frederick Koerner  Massachusetts General Hospital  who made the following comments   The underlying tissue in this core biopsy specimen contains a collection of debris and histiocytes  which one might see as a consequence of rupture of a duct  A uniform population of atypical epithelial cells fills and distends the acini in an adjacent lobules  The cells appear enlarged and they contain slightly enlarged nuclei  homogeneous chromatin  inconspicuous nucleoli  and modest amounts of eosinophilic cytoplasm  The cells do not appear polarized nor do they form cribriform spaces  instead  they grow as a sheet of polygonal cells  Although these findings establish the diagnosis of carcinoma in situ  they do not allow me to identify the nature of the malignant cells  The stain for E cadherin presents a complicated picture  The residual luminal cells of the acini show strong  linear staining for the protein  and the residual myoepithelial cells stain with a fainter  granular  and interrupted pattern  Slight proliferation and off center planes of section make the myoepithelial cells seem especially numerous  Among these preexisting benign cells sit the carcinoma cells  which fail to stain for E cadherin  I would therefore conclude that the carcinoma in situ most likely represents lobular carcinoma in situ  Because of the complexity of the immunohistochemical staining pattern and the small size of the focus  it strikes me as prudent to postpone classification of the carcinoma in situ until one can study an excision specimen through   Immunohistochemical staining on the formalin fixed  paraffin embedded block reveals these results for the ductal carcinoma in situ  Estrogen receptor  POSITIVE   10  of nuclei stained  Final Diagnosis by  Richard J  Sampson  M D  Clinical diagnosis   history  Status post right mastectomy in Maine ten years ago for cancer  solid appearing mass at 12 o clock  left breast  previous benign biopsy  left  cancer  complicated cyst  fibroadenoma Tissue Designation  Breast  needle biopsy   left  12 o clock Gross description  The specimen is received in formalin in one container  labeled  guided breast biopsies  left breast  and consists of a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm  aggregate of soft  tan pink  glistening needle biopsies measuring REMOVED_DATE cm  in diameter each  1 agg  all  Dictated by  LV____ signature on file ____DENNIS J  FEEN  M D  REMOVED_DATE   Final  ____ signature on file ____RICHARD SAMPSON  M D  REMOVED_DATE   Final   report_end , 1.
Accession Number  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status  Final Type  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID OPERATION DATE  16 REMOVED_MONTH 95 ACCESSIONED ON  16 REMOVED_MONTH 95 at 13 10 CLINICAL DATA   None Provided                                                                                      FINAL DIAGNOSIS     BREAST  RIGHT   EXCISIONAL BIOPSY  INFILTRATING DUCTAL CARCINOMA  REMOVED_DATE CM  GRADE 3 OF 3  EXTENDING TO AN INKED RESECTION MARGIN  WITH METASTASIS TO AN INTRA PARENCHYMAL LYMPH NODE  FROZEN SECTION DIAGNOSIS IS CONFIRMED  NOTE  In situ carcinoma is not identified  Estrogen and progesterone receptor status will be reported in an addendum  Diagnosis by  Austin L  Vickery  M D  Signed On  21 REMOVED_MONTH 95 at 15 15                                                                                     RECEPTOR IMMUNOHISTOCHEMISTRY Addendum     RESULTS OF STAINING OF FIXED TISSUE FOR HORMONE RECEPTOR PROTEINS BREAST  RIGHT   The tumor cells stain for estrogen receptor protein  The tumor cells stain for progesterone receptor protein  See note  Note  In this laboratory  we used this antibody to stain 100 breast carcinomas prepared in the usual way  paraffin sections  and we compared the results with determinations of estrogen receptor by biochemical assay  91  of tumors that stained with this antibody were positive for estrogen receptors by biochemical assay  96  of tumors that failed to stain with this antibody were negative for estrogen receptors by biochemical assay  When we compared the results of staining of paraffin sections with the results of staining of frozen sections  the predictive value of a positive result was 97  and the predictive value of a negative result was 95   In this laboratory  we also compared the results of staining of 100 routinely processed breast carcinomas with determinations of progesterone receptors by a hormone binding assay  Sixty five percent of tumors that stained with this antibody contained progesterone receptors by the biochemical assay  and 98  of tumors that failed to stain lacked the receptors  THE RESULTS OF IMMUNOHISTOCHEMICAL STAINING FOR PROGESTERONE RECEPTOR PROTEIN ARE FOR INVESTIGATIONAL USE ONLY  THEY MUST NOT BE USED FOR CLINICAL PURPOSES  Added by  Frederick Carl Koerner  M D  Signed On  28 REMOVED_MONTH 95 at 07 50 GROSS DESCRIPTION  Received fresh in the Frozen Section Lab  labeled with the patient s name  unit number  and  Right breast  is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm nodule of breast tissue with a well demarcated  irregular firm and gritty white to gray nodule  REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm in size abutting an inked resection margin  A portion of the suspicious nodule is frozen as frozen section  1  submitted entirely in A through I  Resident  Rex Neal Smith  MD     INTRA OPERATIVE DIAGNOSIS     FROZEN SECTION DIAGNOSIS  1  BREAST RIGHT EXCISION  INFILTRATING DUCTAL CARCINOMA  O R  Resident  FANG FANG  MD O R  Diagnosis by  John H  Eichhorn  M D                                                                                  SOURCE CARE UNIT  SURGICAL DAY CARE   ACC 3 REPORTS TO  Susan E Briggs  M D  PART   RIGHT BREAST  report_end , 0.
