Accession Number  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status  Final Type  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID BREAST IMPLANT Accessioned On  REMOVED_DATE  BRENN  THOMAS  M D  PH D  DIAGNOSIS  by BRENN THOMAS M D  PH D  SPECIMEN LABELED  RIGHT BREAST IMPLANT   Benign breast tissue and adipose tissue with fat necrosis  foreign body giant cell reaction and fibrous pseudocapsule formation  Tissue expander  gross examination only   SPECIMEN LABELED  SILICONE GRANULOMA   Benign breast tissue and adipose tissue with fat necrosis  foreign body giant cell reaction and fibrous pseudocapsule formation  CLINICAL DATA  History  History of silicone breast implants 5 years ago replaced by saline implants  Large silicone dep  have remained  Now exc  of most of silicone granulomas  R o malignancy  Clinical Diagnosis  Calcium deposits around silicone implant TISSUE SUBMITTED   1  Right breast implant  2  Silicone granuloma GROSS DESCRIPTION  by VANGURI VIJAY K M D  The specimen is received fresh in two parts  each labeled with the patient s name and unit number  Part one  labeled   1  right breast implant   consists of an REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE  cm irregular discoid synthetic medical device grossly consistent with a breast implant  Attached to the implant is abundant red yellow focally hemorrhagic lobulated fat with multiple gelatinous filled nodules  ranging from REMOVED_DATE up to 1 0 cm in greatest dimension   The attached soft tissue is removed from the deflated implant  The transparent capsule is grossly intact and displays a moderate amount of tacky gelatinous material  Calcifications are not grossly identified  Photographs are taken  Micro 1  soft tissue with gelatinous nodules  2 frags  RSS Part two  labeled   2  silicone granuloma   consists of multiple fragments of tan yellow fibrous to fatty soft tissue  7 0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm in aggregate   Within the tissue are innumerable gelatinous filled nodules  ranging from REMOVED_DATE  up to REMOVED_DATE cm in greatest dimension   Micro 2  soft tissue  2 frags  RSS dfi Reports to  ERIKSSON  ELOF M D  SPECIMEN TYPE  BREAST IMPLANT ADDITIONAL SPECIMEN TYPE  SOFT TISSUE BIOPSY  report_end , 0.
Accession Number  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status  Final Type  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID BREAST   EXCISIONAL TISSUE W MARGINS Accessioned On  REMOVED_DATE  RICHARDSON  ANDREA  L   M D  PH D  DIAGNOSIS  by RICHARDSON ANDREA L  M D  PH D  1  RIGHT BREAST WIRE LOCALIZE EXCISION FOR CALCIFICATIONS  EXTENSIVE CARCINOMA IN SITU WITH DUCTAL AND LOBULAR FEATURES  solid type   intermediate nuclear grade  with focal necrosis   SEE NOTE   CARCINOMA IN SITU is present throughout 6 of 6 blocks and extends to the inferior  deep  anterior  lateral and medial margins  No invasive carcinoma seen  Calcifications are identified in carcinoma in situ  however  the vast majority of carcinoma in situ does not mark with calcium  2  ADDITIONAL RIGHT BREAST MEDIAL POSTERIOR EXCISION  EXTENDSIVE CARCINOMA IN SITU WITH DUCTAL AND LOBULAR FEATURES  present in 6 of 6 blocks   SEE NOTE   CARCINOMA IN SITU extends to deep  lateral  medial  inferior and anterior margins  No invasive carcinoma seen  NOTE  The in situ process in these excisions were compared to the prior excision performed at Beth Israel Deaconess in February 2001  The in situ process in this specimen is identical to what was seen before  The in situ carcinoma has predominantly lobular features with large dyshesive cells greatly filling and expanding ducts and lobules  Focally  the cells appear to be more cohesive suggestive of ductal features  E cadherin immunohistochemistry is negative throughout most of the in situ process  but shows focal positive staining also consistent with focal ductal features  The cells are larger and of higher nuclear grade than is seen in more typical LCIS  Also  the presence of central necrosis in some of the expanded ducts is a worrisome feature  We interpret these findings to suggest an in situ process with both lobular and ductal features  Whether such lesions behave more like conventional LCIS or like DCIS is not known  Drs  Stuart Schnitt and Susan Lester have also reviewed the case and concur  CLINICAL DATA  History  Microcalcifications at site of previous surgery for DCIS LCIS  treated with wide excision alone REMOVED_DATE  Clinical Diagnosis  Ca   in situ right breast  TISSUE SUBMITTED  1  Needle loc  right breast  2  Additional medial and posterior right breast tissue   1  Ca   in specimen  C  Denison  MD  GROSS DESCRIPTION  by PREISINGER ELIZABETH ANN M D  Accompanying the specimen is a mammogram of a radiograph that reveals breast tissue with a wire localization in place and possible focus of calcifications  The specimen is received fresh in two parts  each labeled with the patient s name and unit number  Part one  labeled   1  right breast   consists of breast biopsy specimen measuring REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm  The specimen is inked per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins  posterior   black  anterior   blue  superior   red  inferior   green  lateral   yellow  medial   orange with a single wire localization in place  The wire is carefully removed  The specimen consists of 50  yellow adipose tissue interspersed with 50  white fibrotic bands  No gross calcifications are observed upon sectioning  The specimen is submitted entirely for examination  Micro REMOVED_DATE  breast tissue near the site of calcification  2 frags  ESS  Micro REMOVED_DATE  remaining breast tissue  6 frags  ESS  Part two  labeled   2  additional medial and posterior margins   consists of a fragment of breast tissue  measuring REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm  The specimen is inked per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins  posterior   black  anterior   blue  superior   red  inferior   green  lateral   yellow  medial   orange  The specimen consists of almost entirely of yellow adipose tissue  The specimen is entirely submitted  Micro REMOVED_DATE  additional breast tissue  entirely submitted  8 frags  ESS  jhl Reports to  LESNIKOSKI  BETH ANN M D  SPECIMEN TYPE  BREAST   EXCISIONAL TISSUE W MARGI