Accession Number  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status  Final Type  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID BREAST BIOPSY W O MARGINS Accessioned On  REMOVED_DATE  BRODSKY  GILBERT  M D  DIAGNOSIS  by BRODSKY GILBERT M D  SPECIMEN LABELED  LEFT BREAST MASS   Predominantly dense breast tissue with mild perilobular and periductal chronic inflammation  There is no evidence of malignancy or atypia  CLINICAL DATA  History  Bloody discharge  left nipple  Operation  Left nipple flap duct resection  TISSUE SUBMITTED  Left breast resection with suture on duct end  O R  CONSULTATION  Left breast mass  Fibrocystic changes with focal dense fibrosis  GROSS DESCRIPTION  by HAIKAL NABILA ABDUL AZIZ M D  The specimen  received unfixed  labeled  left breast mass   is a REMOVED_DATE x 4 x REMOVED_DATE  cm fibrofatty soft tissue fragment with a suture marking duct end  On section  ing there is a white  firm lesion  3 5 x 3 x REMOVED_DATE cm  and a REMOVED_DATE cm hemorrhagic cyst  A representative portion is submitted for frozen section  the frozen section remnant is submitted in 1 cassette designated  FS   The hemorrhagic region is submitted in 2 cassettes designated  H   The rubbery portion is sub  mitted in 9 cassettes designated  R   The fatty portion is submitted in 2 cas  settes designated  F   Reports to  SHIRLEY  ROBERT  LAWRENCE M D  SPECIMEN TYPE  BREAST BIOPSY W O MARGINS  report_end , 0.
Accession Number  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status  CorrectedType  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ACCESSIONED ON  24 MAY 95 at 13 00 CLINICAL DATA   None Provided      REVISED REPORT     Reason for Revision  ADDITIONAL TISSUE RECEIVED Revised by  Dennis Sgroi  M D  Revised on 05 REMOVED_MONTH 95 at 13 51                                                                                     FINAL DIAGNOSIS     BREAST  LEFT  BIOPSY  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   1  Infiltrating ductal carcinoma  REMOVED_DATE cm  as measured on a glass slide  well differentiated  Grade I III   extending to less than one high power field of an inked surface   See Note   2  Intraductal carcinoma  cribriform type  multifocal  three microscopic foci  focally with microcalcifications   See Note  3  Atypical intraductal carcinoma  multifocal  present in 9 of 13 microscopic slides  4  Intraductal hyperplasia  diffuse  5  Radial scar  6  Fibrocystic changes with microcysts  sclerosing adenosis  intralobular fibrosis  apocrine metaplasia and microcalcifications  Note  Blood vascular and lymphatic invasion are not seen  A single focus of ductal carcinoma in situ is associated with the infiltrating carcinoma  while the other two foci are separate from the infiltrating component  The tumor cells stain for progesterone receptor protein  The stain for estrogen receptor protein appears focally positive  however  increased background staining precludes a definitive diagnosis  A repeat estrogen receptor stain is recommended  Diagnosis by  Dennis Sgroi  M D  Signed On  15 REMOVED_MONTH 95 at 08 41                                                                                     IMMUNOHISTOCHEMISTRY Addendum     RESULTS OF STAINING OF FIXED TISSUE FOR HORMONE RECEPTOR PROTEINS BREAST  LEFT   THE TUMOR CELLS DO NOT STAIN FOR ESTROGEN RECEPTOR PROTEIN  THE TUMOR CELLS STAIN FOR PROGESTERONE RECEPTOR PROTEIN  SEE NOTE  Note  In this laboratory  we used this antibody to stain 100 breast carcinomas prepared in the usual way  paraffin sections  and we compared the results with determinations of estrogen receptor by biochemical assay  91  of tumors that stained with this antibody were positive for estrogen receptors by biochemical assay  96  of tumors that failed to stain with this antibody were negative for estrogen receptors by biochemical assay  When we compared the results of staining of paraffin sections with the results of staining of frozen sections  the predictive value of a positive result was 97  and the predictive value of a negative result was 95   In this laboratory  we also compared the results of staining of 100 routinely processed breast carcinomas with determinations of progesterone receptors by a hormone binding assay  Sixty five percent of tumors that stained with this antibody contained progesterone receptors by the biochemical assay  and 98  of tumors that failed to stain lacked the receptors  THE RESULTS OF IMMUNOHISTOCHEMICAL STAINING FOR PROGESTERONE RECEPTOR PROTEIN ARE FOR INVESTIGATIONAL USE ONLY  THEY MUST NOT BE USED FOR CLINICAL PURPOSES  Added by  Dennis Sgroi  M D  Signed On  15 REMOVED_MONTH 95 at 08 42                                                                                 SOURCE CARE UNIT  MGH OUTSIDE LOCATION REPORTS TO  Michele Annette Gadd  M D  Simon Powell  M D  OUTSIDE INSTITUTION  MAINE MEDICAL CENTER OUTSIDE PATHOLOGIST  Allen L  Pusch  MD MAINE MEDICAL CENTER  PATH DEPT  22 BRAMHALL ST  PORTLAND  ME REMOVED_CASE_ID PART   LEFT BREAST BIOPSY  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Date of Procedure  06 MAR 1995 Slides  23 Blocks  0  report_end , 0.
