Accession Number  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status  Final Type  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID BREAST CORE NEEDLE BIOPSY IES  Accessioned On  REMOVED_DATE  LESTER  SUSAN  M D  PH D  DIAGNOSIS  by LESTER SUSAN M D  PH D  LEFT BREAST  12 00  11 G ULTRASOUND GUIDED CORE NEEDLE BIOPSY FOR A SOLID  LOBULATED NODULE  INVASIVE CARCINOMA  POORLY DIFFERENTIATED  at least REMOVED_DATE cm in size  SEE NOTE   No in situ carcinoma is seen  Immunoperoxidise studies are performed on formalin fixed tissue  ER   POSITIVE PR   NEGATIVE REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID NEU   NEGATIVE GCDFP   FOCALLY POSITIVE NOTE  The carcinoma consists of sheets and nests of cells with large pleomorphic nuclei and abundant cytoplasm  The carcinoma is histologically similar to areas of the patient s ovarian carcinoma  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  and colon metastasis  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Compared to the ovarian carcinoma  the breast carcinoma demonstrates slightly more GCDFP immunoreactivity and diminished progesterone receptor and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID neu positivity  Both carcinomas are immunoreactive for estrogen receptor  Although metastatic ovarian carcinoma to the breast is favored  a separate breast primary carcinoma cannot be excluded  SURGICAL ADDENDUM  by LESTER SUSAN M D  PH D  The core needle biopsy as well as the subsequent breast excision  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  and prior ovarian excision  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  were reviewed by Dr  Paul Peter Rosen at Cornell Medical Center  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  REMOVED_DATE   and the following is his consultation report  The slides of ovarian and breast tumors from the above named patient were reviewed by Dr  Hoda in my absence and are now re reviewed at the request of the patient  Slides  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  show a high grade  solid and papillary adenocarcinoma of the ovary  predominantly serous type  Slides  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  a needle core biopsy  and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  a lymph node and breast biopsy  show poorly differentiated adenocarcinoma with a modest lymphoplasmacytic reaction in breast tissue  There is no obvious intraductal carcinoma at the site of the approximately 6mm breast tumor  Although  the carcinoma in the breast bears a resemblance to the ovarian carcinoma  there are some notable differences  The structure of the breast tumor is entirely solid and virtually syncytial  lacking the papillary and secondary gland features of the ovarian carcinoma  Mitoses are more numerous in the ovarian tumor  35 per 10 high power fields  than in the breast lesion  5 per 10 high power fields   There are also subtle cytologic differences since the breast tumor is composed of cells with more uniform round nuclei that have prominent  central nucleoli whereas carcinoma cells in the ovary have nuclei with more varied shapes and absent or small heterogeneous nucleoli  Sections of a lymph node do not reveal metastatic carcinoma  Some of the cytologic variation might be related to laboratory techniques  since the specimens were processed by separate institutions  there is enough difference to leave me unconvinced that the breast harbors metastatic ovarian carcinoma and to rule out the possibility of a primary mammary carcinoma  Various reports for these specimens refer to marker immunostains done on the ovarian and breast tumors  These could be crucial to the diagnosis but none have been supplied for me to review  a situation I hope you will address  In this regard  there are additional marker studies which can be compared between the two lesions and for this purpose I would like to receive blocks  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  2  and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  6  or fifteen unstained sections from each suitable for immunohistochemistry   NB  Unstained slides were sent to Dr  Rosen after his report was issued   Additional consultation report  9 21 01   Immunostains for hormone receptors  ER  PR and androgen  performed on the breast tumor show nuclear reactivity for ER      but no reactivity for PR     or androgen receptor      The ovarian tumor shows nuclear reactivity for both ER      and PR       and absence of staining for androgen receptor      The prognostic marker Her 2 neu was negative in both the breast and ovarian tumors  A proliferative marker  Ki 67  showed strong reactivity       in both the breast and ovarian tumors  An immunostain for gross cystic disease fluid protein  Brst  shows absence of staining     in both the breast and ovarian tumors  a result that differs from the findings in your report REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  An immunostain  Mito 113  shows reactivity       in the breast and ovarian tumors  A p53 immunostain could be performed only on the ovarian tumor as we did not have any more unstained slides from the breast tumor  It shows strong p53       reactivity within the ovarian tumor  The pattern of reactivity for the above cited markers is similar  except for PR  in both tumors and therefore of limited assistance in assessing the breast lesion  The marker results for the breast lesion are consistent with a primary breast tumor  In my opinion  based on the histomorphology of both tumors  I am inclined to favor the breast tumor as a primary and not metastases from the ovary  CLINICAL DATA  History  Solid nodule  L 12 00 breast  History of ovarian cancer  Operation  11 gauge ultrasound guided  Operative findings  Solid lobulated nodule  Clinical Diagnosis  FA vs IDC  TISSUE SUBMITTED  Left breast tissue in a green cassette  GROSS DESCRIPTION  by CARTER MARK RYAN M D  The specimen is received in formalin  labeled with the patient s name  unit number  and  left breast   in a single light green cassette labeled  left   and consists of multiple  soft  fragmented  yellow to white tissue cores  2 6 x 0 6 x REMOVED_DATE cm in aggregate   Micro 1  light green cassette labeled  left   multi frags  ESS  kks Reports to  SMITH  DARRELL  NOLAN M D  SPECIMEN TYPE  BREAST CORE NEEDLE BIOPSY IES   report_end , 1.
