Accession Number  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status  Final Type  Surgical Pathology Specimen Type  Consult material Procedure Date  Ordering Provider  CAPRICE CHRISTIAN GREENBERG M D  CASE  BS 08 REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID PATIENT  REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME Resident  Kevin B Long  M D  Pathologist  Jane E  Brock  M B   B S   Ph D  PATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS  CONSULT SLIDES FROM WINCHESTER HOSPITAL  WINCHESTER  MA  RIGHT BREAST  EXCISION FOR A MASS  S 07 REMOVED_CASE_ID  REMOVED_DATE    INVASIVE CARCINOMA  poorly differentiated  most consistent with a MALIGNANT ADENOMYOEPITHELIOMA  REMOVED_DATE cm by report  see NOTE   No lymphovascular invasion is present  Mitoses in tumor cells number up to REMOVED_DATE HPF in the myoepithelial component and up to REMOVED_DATE HPF in the epithelial component  Focal tumor necrosis is present  block 5   No carcinoma in situ is present  Tumor is less than REMOVED_DATE cm from the lateral  yellow  margin and REMOVED_DATE cm from the inferior  green  margin  Tumor is REMOVED_DATE cm from the medial  orange  and superior  blue  margins  Anterior and posterior margins are   REMOVED_DATE cm  Immunohistochemistry was performed at BWH with the following results  ESTROGEN RECEPTOR NEGATIVE PROGESTERONE RECEPTOR POSITIVE  epithelial cells only  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID NEU NEGATIVE  0 to 1   Immunohistochemistry performed at the outside hospital and reviewed here  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID POSITIVE  in myoepithelial cells predominantly and rare epithelial cells  NOTE  The tumor has an unusual biphasic morphology composed of clusters of epithelial cells distributed evenly throughout a background of spindle cells  The epithelial cells form tubular glands and nests which are PR positive and keratin positive  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Pan K  Cam REMOVED_DATE and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID 6   The spindle cells range from rounded to a more spindled appearance  have high grade nuclei  and these cells stain with keratin  Pan K   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID 6  as well as the myoepithelial markers p63 and calponin consistent with myoepithelial differentiation  The tumor has a lobulated border which is irregular and infiltrative  The staining pattern excludes an unusual phyllodes tumor  A malignant adenomyoepithelioma is favored given the distinctive morphological appearance and supporting immunostains  Although criteria which predict local recurrence and the development of distant metastases are not definitively established  available reports in the literature suggest that the presence of myoepithelial and epithelial nuclear pleomorphism and atypia  along with mitoses   REMOVED_DATE HPF  necrosis  and a lobulated or infiltrative border favors a diagnosis of  malignant adenomyoepithelioma   The two largest series have been published by Drs  Tavassoli and Page  see references below   Dr  S  Lester    Dr  A  Richardson have also reviewed this case and agree with the diagnosis  References  Tavassoli FA  Myoepithelial lesions of the breast  Myoepitheliosis  adenomyoepithelioma and myoepithelial carcinoma  Am J Surg Pathol 1991  15 6  554 568  McLaren BK et al  Adenomyoepithelioma  a clinical  histologic and immunohistologic evaluation of a series of related lesions  Am J Surg Pathol 2005  REMOVED_DATE  CLINICAL DATA  History  Breast ca Clinical Diagnosis  TISSUE SUBMITTED  Consult slides  GROSS DESCRIPTION  Received from Winchester Hospital  Department of Pathology  41 Highland Avenue  Winchester  MA REMOVED_CASE_ID are seventeen  17  stained slides  10 H E  7 IHC  labeled  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   with the H E slides sublabeled  FSA   and  1  through  9   corresponding to breast mass  right  excision  5 3 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm  obtained on 12 06 2007  according to the accompanying pathology report bearing the patient s name  Subsequently received on REMOVED_DATE  is one paraffin block labeled  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  and sublabled  3   Subsequently received on REMOVED_DATE  is one paraffin block labeled  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  and 