Accession Number  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status  Final Type  Surgical Pathology Specimen Type  Consult material Procedure Date  Ordering Provider  ANN S LACASCE M D  CASE  BS 08 REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID PATIENT  REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME Resident  Travis J Hollmann  M D   Ph D  Pathologist  Geraldine S Pinkus  M D  PATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS  CONSULT SLIDES FROM UMASS MEMORIAL MEDICAL CENTER  WORCESTER  MA RIGHT BREAST  NEEDLE CORE BIOPSY  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  REMOVED_DATE    ATYPICAL LYMPHOID INFILTRATE  SUSPICIOUS FOR INVOLVEMENT BY NON HODGKIN LYMPHOMA  B CELL TYPE  see note  The tissue sections show multiple fragments of fibroadipose tissue  scattered glandular elements and a focal  dense lymphoid infiltrate with crush artifact  The lymphoid cells are predominantly small to intermediate in size with irregular nuclei  partially condensed chromatin and inconspicuous nucleoli however scattered intermediate to large nucleolated forms are also apparent  The larger forms are particularly apparent at one tissue edge  Histiocytes  small lymphocytes and occasional mast cells are also seen  Immunohistochemical studies performed on paraffin embedded material at the referring institution and reviewed at BWH reveal the majority of the lymphoid cells are REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID positive B cells with numerous interspersed REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID positive T cells  A subset of the B cells appears to express REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  The T cells express REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Weak bcl 6 positivity is noted for scattered cells  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID is negative  Immunostains for immunoglobulin kappa and lambda light chains are not definitive due to high background  The Ki 67 proliferation index is variable  with an average index of 30   and focal areas approaching 50   however  much of the tissue is not present on the level used for study  NOTE  Overall findings are of an ATYPICAL LYMPHOID INFILTRATE SUSPICIOUS FOR INVOLVEMENT BY NON HODGKIN LYMPHOMA  B CELL TYPE  While larger forms are noted  due to the small size of the specimen and crush artifact  accurate grading and subclassification is not possible  Clinical correlation is advised and  if clinically indicated  re biopsy is recommended  with tissue allocation for routine studies as well as for flow cytometric and molecular studies  for more precise evaluation of this process   Clinical  35 year old female with right breast mass  CLINICAL DATA  History  B cell non Hodgkin lymphoma  TISSUE SUBMITTED  Consult slides  GROSS DESCRIPTION  Received from UMASS Memorial Medical Center  Three Biotech  One Innovation Drive  Worcester  MA  REMOVED_CASE_ID  are fourteen glass slides labeled  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   corresponding to right breast  needle core biopsy  H E x2  kappa  lambda  bcl 6  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Ki 67  Neg x2   from a procedure performed on REMOVED_DATE  according to the accompanying surgical pathology report bearing the patient s name  By his her signature below  the senior physician certifies that he she personally conducted a microscopic examination   gross only  exam if so stated  of the described specimen s  and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis es  related thereto  Final Diagnosis by Geraldine S Pinkus M D   Electronically signed on 3 12 2008  report_end , 0.
