oadipose breast tissue  2 6 cm medial to lateral  REMOVED_DATE cm superior to inferior  and REMOVED_DATE cm anterior to posterior  with a short suture designating the superior margin  and a long suture designating the lateral margin  There is a needle localization wire in placed  which enters through the lateral aspect of the specimen and exits through the medial aspect of the specimen  The specimen is inked per pathology protocol  superior red  inferior green  medial orange  lateral yellow  anterior blue  posterior black  and serially sectioned from mediolateral  The cut surface is tan yellow and homogeneous with focal area of dense white fibrous tissue constituting approximately greater than 95  of the breast parenchyma  No discrete lesions are grossly identified  The specimen is submitted in its entirety  Micro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Medial margin  2 frags  ESS  Micro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Sequential cross sections  medial to lateral  2 frags each  ESS  Micro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Lateral margin  2 frags  ESS  Part B   medial margin   consists of an unoriented irregular tan yellow lobulated portion of fibroadipose tissue  1 2 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm   One surface of the specimen is concave and slightly ragged    old   black   and the opposite surface is convex and smooth    new   blue   The specimen is serially sectioned and the cut surface is tan yellow and homogeneous with focal areas of dense white fibrous tissue constituting approximately 70  of the breast parenchyma  No discrete lesions or areas of disruption are grossly identified  The specimen is submitted in its entirety  Micro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Sequential cross sections  2 frags each  ESS  sek lp CASE NUMBER  REMOVED_CASE_ID  By his her signature below  the senior physician certifies that he she personally conducted a microscopic examination   gross only  exam if so stated  of the described specimen s  and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis es  related thereto  Final Diagnosis by Deborah A Dillon M D   Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE  SURGICAL ADDENDUM  6 06 08   The patient presented with calcifications associated with a REMOVED_DATE to REMOVED_DATE cm lesion described as architectural distortion or an irregular mass  The first wire localized excision  specimen  1 above  did not contain either the calcifications or the mass  The additional specimen designated  medial margin  contained the calcifications and a possible portion of the mass  The slides show a REMOVED_DATE cm focus of the larger complex sclerosing lesion  CSL  removed in the subsequent excision  The CSL is involved by carcinoma in situ  CIS   As stated in the original report above  the CIS is difficult to classify due to the small area of the lesion available for evaluation  The subsequent excision reveals a 1 cm CSL associated with biopsy site changes  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   The CSL correlates with the original mass detected by radiology that was targeted for excision  The CSL is partially involved by CIS that can be better characterized  as there is a larger area available for evaluation  The histologic appearance of the cells and the absence of E cadherin support classification as LCIS  The cells are positive for estrogen and progesterone receptors and negative for REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID neu  In the context of the subsequent excision  the CIS present in  specimen  2  medial margin  above is compatible with a diagnosis of LCIS  Case reviewed by Dr  Susan Lester  who concurs  This carcinoma would be classified as AJCC  6th edition  Tis  LCIS   Addendum  1 by Deborah A Dillon M D   Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE   report_end , 1.
