Accession Number  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status  Final Type  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID BREAST   EXCISIONAL TISSUE W MARGINS Accessioned On  REMOVED_DATE  BRODSKY  GILBERT  M D  DIAGNOSIS  by BRODSKY GILBERT M D  SKIN   LEFT BREAST TISSUE   RE EXCISION  INVASIVE POORLY DIFFERENTIATED CARCINOMA  in deep dermis subcutis  consistent with breast primary  see NOTE   Tumor is present in the mid section of the ellipse  block  3  and is present at the deep resection margin  Cicatrix and foreign body reaction to suture  NOTE  Immunohistochemical studies were performed on a recent prior specimen  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   SURGICAL ADDENDUM  by BRODSKY GILBERT M D  The side resection margins are free of tumor  CLINICAL DATA  History  A 33 year old female with lump   left breast  Operation  Re excision Clinical Diagnosis  S P left breast cancer  mastectomy  reconstruction TISSUE SUBMITTED  Left breast tissue  re excision    suture   3 o clock medial GROSS DESCRIPTION  by DAVID FRANK SAMUEL M D  PH D  The specimen is received fresh in one part  labeled with the patient s name and unit number only  and consists of a single skin ellipse and attached subcutaneous tissue  6 x 1 x REMOVED_DATE cm   There is a single suture in the specimen  The skin contains a 5 cm well healed surgical scar  The suture is designated 12 o clock by the pathologist  and the 3 o clock margin is inked blue while the 9 o clock margin is inked in black  The specimen is serially sectioned and entirely submitted for histologic analysis  Micro 1  skin tip from 12 o clock margin  1 frag  ESS Micro 2  6 o clock skin tip  1 frag  ESS Micro REMOVED_DATE  serial sections from REMOVED_DATE o clock  mult frags  ESS fsd Reports to  BREUING  KARL  HEINZ  ULRICH M D  SPECIMEN TYPE  BREAST   EXCISIONAL TISSUE W MARGINS  report_end , 1.
Accession Number  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status  Final Type  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID BREAST   EXCISIONAL TISSUE W MARGINS Accessioned On  REMOVED_DATE  LESTER  SUSAN  M D  PH D  DIAGNOSIS  by LESTER SUSAN M D  PH D  RIGHT BREAST  WIRE LOCALIZATION FOR CALCIFICATIONS  CARCINOMA IN SITU  with ductal and lobular features  is present in two of 16 blocks  foci measuring REMOVED_DATE and REMOVED_DATE cm  blocks 12 and 13  levels examined   see note   No calcifications or necrosis are associated with CIS  Immunoperoxidase studies are performed with the following results for CIS  block 13   E cadherin NEGATIVE Estrogen receptor POSITIVE Progesterone receptor POSITIVE REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID neu NEGATIVE  1   Carcinoma in situ is   REMOVED_DATE cm from the medial margin  two blocks  and 0 2 cm from the inferior margin  CIS is not seen close to the other margins  Calcifications are associated with apocrine adenosis and columnar cell change with atypia  e g  blocks 8 and 10   Note  The CIS has the architectural pattern of LCIS with pagetoid spread into an adjacent duct and lacks e cadherin  However  the nuclei are enlarged and pleomorphic and not typical for LCIS  Thus this lesion is best classified as CIS with mixed features  Selected slides were also reviewed by Dr  Stuart Schnitt who concurs with this interpretation  This carcinoma would be classified as AJCC  6th edition  Tis NX MX  CLINICAL DATA  History  42 year old female whose REMOVED_DATE mammograms from Lowell General Hospital demonstrate right breast UOQ calcifications for which Ms Garneau prefers excision  Operation  Right breast wire localization biopsy Clinical Diagnosis  Abnormal right mammogram TISSUE SUBMITTED   1  Right breast tissue  short stitch superior  long stitch lateral  whip stitch deep margin  RADIOLOGY  Calcifications in specimen  Dr  Meyer GROSS DESCRIPTION  by CHEN JEY HSIN M D  PH D  The specimen is received fresh  labeled with the patient s name  unit number  and   1  right breast tissue mammo  short stitch superior  long stitch lateral  whip deep margin   and consists of a fragment of soft tissue  5 5 x REMOVED_DATE x 1 5 cm  oriented by a short stitch marking the superior surface  long stitch marking the lateral surface  and a whip stitch marking the deep surface  The specimen is received inked by the surgeon per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins  posterior black  anterior blue  superior red  inferior green  lateral yellow  medial orange  Also received within the specimen is a wire not contained within the specimen  Accompanying the specimen is a mammogram which demonstrates a broad area of calcifications within the specimen  The specimen is serially sectioned from lateral to medial revealing a pink tan lobulated tissue which encompasses approximately 70  of the specimen  The specimen is entirely submitted  Micro REMOVED_DATE  lateral most slice  perpendicular  9 frags  ESS Micro REMOVED_DATE  serial sections from lateral to medial  12 frags  ESS Micro REMOVED_DATE  medial most slice  perpendicular  7 frags  ESS jhc Reports to  RHEI  ESTHER M D  SPECIMEN TYPE  BREAST   EXCISIONAL TISSUE W MARGINS  report_end , 0.
Accession Number  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status  Final Type  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID BREAST   EXCISIONAL TISSUE W MARGINS Accessioned On  REMOVED_DATE  GLICKMAN  JONATHAN  NEIL  M D  PH D  DIAGNOSIS  by GLICKMAN JONATHAN NEIL M D  PH D  SPECIMEN LABELED  RIGHT BREAST  MASS   Benign breast tissue with mild interlobular fibrosis and a one focus of lactational change  There is no evidence of malignancy  CLINICAL DATA  History  Right breast mass  Operation  Right breast biopsy  Clinical Diagnosis  Right breast mass  TISSUE SUBMITTED  1  Right breast mass  GROSS DESCRIPTION  by BARRY MARC The specimen is received fresh in one part  labeled with the patient s name  unit number  and   1  right breast mass   consists of two  2  fragments of fibrofatty tissue  REMOVED_DATE and REMOVED_DATE cm in diameter  Cut section shows focal fibrosis and the specimen is submitted in toto for microscopic examination  Micro 1  smaller fragment  1 frag  ESS  Micro 2  larger fragment bisected  2 frags  ESS  mab Reports to  OSTEEN  ROBERT  T  M D  SPECIMEN TYPE  BREAST   EXCISIONAL TISSUE W MARGINS  report_end , 0.
REMOVED_CASE_ID HAMWEY ANGELA 46 F  REG REF REMOVED_DATE    REMOVED_CASE_ID  WIC AUGUST ALLISON M D                                                                                             Specimen  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID REMOVED_CASE_ID SOUT Received  REMOVED_DATE  1600 Spec Type    Clinical diagnosis   history  Suspicious clustered microcalcifications of upper outer quadrant of right breast and solid nodules of upper inner quadrant near nipple   1  ductal carcinoma in situ vs fibrocystic changes   2  solid nodule    fibroadenoma Edited by  REMOVED_DATE    0659 PAT MURJ Gross description  The specimen is received in formalin in two containers  Specimen A labeled  guided breast biopsies  right breast  lateral  1  10 o clock  and consists of a 1 x 1 x REMOVED_DATE cm  aggregate of tan white  firm  glistening needle biopsies admixed with blood clot measuring REMOVED_DATE to REMOVED_DATE cm  in diameter  1 agg  all  Specimen B labeled  guided breast biopsies  right breast  12 30  and consists of four firm  yellowish white  glistening needle biopsies measuring REMOVED_DATE cm  in length x REMOVED_DATE cm  in diameter to REMOVED_DATE cm  in length x REMOVED_DATE cm  in diameter  4X all  Dictated by  LV Edited by  REMOVED_DATE    0815 PAT MURJ DIAGNOSIS  A  Right breast  10 o clock  needle core biopsy  Atypical ductal hyperplasia  see note   No microcalcifications identified  multiple level sections were obtained  B  Right breast  12 30  needle core biopsy  DIAGNOSIS   Continued  Fibroadenoma  Edited by  REMOVED_DATE    1038 PAT MURJ DESCRIPTIVE COMMENT  NOTE  Specimen A  needle core biopsy at 10 o clock  was sent to Dr  Frederick Koerner at Massachusetts General Hospital  He concurs with the diagnosis  His note and recommendation for excision reads as follows   The sections of this core biopsy specimen show a background of slight fibrocystic changes  Atypical epithelial cells occupy a small duct and associated lobules  Those in the duct have columnar shapes and they possess slightly enlarged nuclei and eosinophilic apical cytoplasm  I would classify these cells as atypical columnar cells and the proliferation as an especially mild form of atypical ductal hyperplasia  The cells in the nearby acini display more pronounced atypia  They look large and they contain large nuclei  pale  homogeneous chromatin  small nucleoli  and abundant  eosinophilic cytoplasm  The cells pile up slightly and  thereby  fill and slightly distend the glands  I regard these cells  too  as atypical ductal cells and the focus as atypical ductal hyperplasia  It could represent a small portion of a ductal carcinoma in situ  A few other acini distant from this region contain atypical epithelial cells that look a bit different  They appear slightly smaller  the nuclei look smaller and darker and the nucleoli seem less evident  moreover  the cytoplasm has a grey blue color  I cannot confidently identify the nature of these cells  but I suspect that they may represent small regions of lobular neoplasia  atypical lobular hyperplasia   In light of the presence of these atypical epithelial proliferations and the absence of calcifications  I would suggest that the patient and her physicians seriously consider an excision of this region of the right breast   Final Diagnosis by  Nicole A  Mihok  M D  Edited by  REMOVED_DATE    1044 PAT MURJ Notes  REMOVED_DATE  7 slides ret d from NEMC  jam  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  REMOVED_DATE  NEMC report rec d  djd REMOVED_DATE  Per Dr  Homa Safaii  NEMC Path  sent 7  A s  to her  dg REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID REMOVED_DATE  7 slides ret d from MGH  djd CR REMOVED_DATE  Path report faxed from MGH  jam REMOVED_DATE  Report  letter  faxed from MGH  jam REMOVED_DATE  7 slides  all A  to Dr  Frederick Koerner  MGH Pathology for opinion per NAM djd CO Edited by  REMOVED_DATE    1615 PAT DORD REMOVED_DATE    0644 PAT MURJ REMOVED_DATE    0742 PAT MURJ Notes   Continued  REMOVED_DATE    1034 PAT DORD REMOVED_DATE    1005 PAT GILD REMOVED_DATE    0805