Accession Number  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status  FinalType  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID OPERATION DATE  23 REMOVED_MONTH 96 ACCESSIONED ON  23 REMOVED_MONTH 96 at 11 02 CLINICAL DATA  Silk short stitch inferior  long stitch medial                                                                                      FINAL DIAGNOSIS     BREAST  RIGHT   BIOPSY  SOLID PAPILLARY CARCINOMA  INVASIVE  PRESENT REMOVED_DATE CM FROM THE MEDIAL MARGIN  NOTE  The tumor is REMOVED_DATE cm in greatest dimension  however the amount of invasion is difficult to ascertain  Definite lymphatic and blood vascular invasion is not seen  ER and PR will be reported in an addendum  The frozen section diagnosis is confirmed  Diagnosis by  Dennis Sgroi  M D  Signed On  26 REMOVED_MONTH 96 at 13 04 Consulting Pathologist s   Frederick Carl Koerner  M D  Robert Edward Scully  M D                                                                                      ELECTRON MICROSCOPY Addendum          ELECTRON MICROSCOPY   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID      RIGHT BREAST  SOLID PAPILLARY CARCINOMA WITH NEUROENDOCRINE DIFFERENTIATION  SEE NOTE  NOTE  The neoplastic cells are closely apposed and interconnected by intermediate junctions and desmosomes  Focally they are palisaded around small blood vessels  forming pseudorosettes  The non vascular poles of the cells often end on lumens and have tight junctions and microvilli  A striking feature of the cytoplasm is numerous dense core  neurosecretory type granules  In addition  some cells have larger serous or exocrine type granules  but no true mucus containing granules are found  Some of the cells contain tonofibrils  indicating partial squamous differentiation  A rare intracytoplasmic lumen  often seen in mammary ductal and lobular cells  is also identified  Added by  G  Richard Dickersin  M D  Signed On  30 REMOVED_MONTH 96 at 15 32     RECEPTOR IMMUNOHISTOCHEMISTRY Addendum     RESULTS OF STAINING OF FIXED TISSUE FOR HORMONE RECEPTOR PROTEINS BREAST  RIGHT   The tumor cells stain for estrogen receptor protein  A few tumor cells stain for progesterone receptor protein  See note  Note  In this laboratory  we used this antibody to stain 100 breast carcinomas prepared in the usual way  paraffin sections  and we compared the results with determinations of estrogen receptor by biochemical assay  91  of tumors that stained with this antibody were positive for estrogen receptors by biochemical assay  96  of tumors that failed to stain with this antibody were negative for estrogen receptors by biochemical assay  When we compared the results of staining of paraffin sections with the results of staining of frozen sections  the predictive value of a positive result was 97  and the predictive value of a negative result was 95   In this laboratory  we also compared the results of staining of 100 routinely processed breast carcinomas with determinations of progesterone receptors by a hormone binding assay  Sixty five percent of tumors that stained with this antibody contained progesterone receptors by the biochemical assay  and 98  of tumors that failed to stain lacked the receptors  Added by  Frederick Carl Koerner  M D  Signed On  03 MAY 96 at 15 26 GROSS DESCRIPTION  Received fresh in the Frozen Section Lab  labeled with the patient s name and unit number only  is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm piece of soft tissue  with a short stitch designating the inferior margin and a long stitch designating the medial margin  The specimen is inked with black medially and red laterally and sectioned to reveal a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm well circumscribed  white tan  fleshy  firm  grossly encapsulated nodule  REMOVED_DATE cm from the closest inked resection margin  A section of the nodule is frozen as frozen section  1  and kept frozen  Samples of the nodule are saved for EM  The remainder are submitted in cassettes A through C  from superior to inferior  Resident  RAFFAELE PENNELLA  MD     INTRA OPERATIVE DIAGNOSIS     FXDX  1  BREAST  RIGHT   BIOPSY  DUCTAL PROLIFERATIVE LESION WITH MITOSES  PROBABLY LOW GRADE INTRADUCTAL PAPILLARY CARCINOMA  AWAIT PERMANENTS  O R  Resident  CHIN LEE WU  MD  PhD O R  Diagnosis by  Eugene Jerome Mark  M D  O R  Consulting Pathologist s   Robert Edward Scully  M D                                                                                  SOURCE CARE UNIT  SAME DAY SURGICAL UNIT   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID REPORTS TO  Edwin Lee Carter  M D  PART   BREAST  RIGHT   EXCISION, 1.
